*
* START-HISTORY:
* 25 Oct 26 Windows port - from Linux 04f1eaa - the screen library as of S.75 TK-0.13 (containers and viewports, slider, selector, colour, accelerators, table styles)
* 22 Oct 26 dm  New (S.75, TK-0.8).
* 22 Oct 26 dm  The look is [off | ON] (see $sl_slider), the ATTR clause names the style of the word in force and the WORDS clause the two words.
* 22 Oct 26 dm  PLACE: where the label is (left by default).
* END-HISTORY
*
* START-DESCRIPTION:
*
*   SCREEN SLIDER f, id, text {AT x,y} {VALUE v} {ATTR style} {WORDS off,on} {PLACE where} {COLOR fg,bg} {ACCEL key}
*
*   is compiled into
*
*   call !screen_slider(f, id, text, x, y, value, attr, offword, onword, place, fg, bg, accel, ret, status)
*
*     f id     the form and the widget's name in it (as SCREEN LABEL)
*     text     shown after the switch
*     x y      the place of the switch from the form's top left corner, default 1,1
*     value    1 to start on (default 0)
*     attr     the style of the word in force, any style of $sl_theme (bold, highlight, header, error, underline ...); default bold, so the word is in capitals AND
*              in the style the theme gives bold (a colour in a colour theme); window draws it as the form's own, so that only the capitals show the state
*     words    two words, the first for the off state and the second for the on state: WORDS 'left','right' draws [left | RIGHT] and [LEFT | right]; default
*              off and on
*     place    where the label is, in relation to the switch: left (the default: text [off | ON]), right ([off | ON] text), above or below (two rows)
*     status   0 added; 1 not (ELSE)
*
*   The switch is drawn [off | ON] when it is on and [OFF | on] when it is off (with the words the program chose, the one in force in capitals), with its label
*   where place says.  The space bar slides it the other way, Left sets it off, Right sets it on, a click on a word sets that state and a click on the label
*   slides it the other way.
*   SCREEN GET f, id answers 1 or 0; SCREEN SET f, id VALUE v sets it; a change is a change event of SCREEN WAIT, as for a check box.
*
*   COLOR fg,bg gives the switch and its label their own colours (see SCREEN ENTRY) until the switch has the focus; the word in force is still in the style ATTR names.
*
*   ACCEL key gives the switch a key that reaches it wherever the focus is (see SCREEN ENTRY): f1 to f12, ctrl-a to ctrl-z, alt-a to alt-z or alt-0 to alt-9.  The key
*   takes the focus to the switch and slides it the other way (a change event).  A letter marked with & in the text (&Shell) is also its Alt-letter.
*
* END-DESCRIPTION
*
* START-CODE

$internal
subroutine screen_slider(f, id, text, ax, ay, value, attr, offword, onword, place, fg, bg, accel, ret, status)
$catalog !screen_slider

   ret = ''
   status = 1
   w = collection()
   w{'id'} = id
   w{'text'} = text
   x = 1
   y = 1
   if ax # '' then x = ax + 0
   if ay # '' then y = ay + 0
   w{'x'} = x
   w{'y'} = y
   w{'value'} = value
   w{'attr'} = attr
   w{'offw'} = offword
   w{'onw'} = onword
   w{'place'} = place
   call $sl_slider('init', w, '', '', r)
   call $sl_color(w, fg, bg, cst)
   if cst # 0 then return
   call $sl_accel(w, accel, ast)
   if ast # 0 then return
   call $sl_wadd(f, w, ok)
   if ok then status = 0

   return
end

* END-CODE
